
1. Meeting Objective

The discussion focused on defining the future-state architecture for the Azure migration, while keeping the immediate implementation scope limited to the originally identified applications, TOAP, and Knowledge Base. The first two application migrations should establish a “guiding light” / reference architecture for future enterprise applications.

2. Key Discussion Points
A. Azure & Databricks Architecture
The team will evaluate 2–3 architecture options for Azure and specifically for Databricks.
Options must be compared using measurable/meaningful parameters such as:
Cost
Scalability
Latency
Performance
Security
Reliability
Migration complexity/effort
Maintainability
Operational complexity
Future extensibility
The team should provide pros, cons, assumptions, and a recommendation for each approach.
Databricks architecture should be evaluated specifically from a scalability and future-growth perspective.
The recommendation should be based on the client's existing database, code repositories, applications, and expected future-state requirements.
B. Current State → Future State

The team needs to understand and document:

Where applications are currently hosted.
Where data currently resides.
Whether the future state should use one database or multiple databases.
What will replace Supabase.
Where permissions/authorization will live.
Where files, attachments, and Blob Storage will live.
How Azure and Databricks will manage access and permissions.
How existing redundant tables can potentially be consolidated during migration.

The Azure lift-and-shift should be used as an opportunity to consolidate redundant tables, where there is a strong business and technical justification.

C. Database Consolidation / Pressure Testing
Do not automatically reproduce every existing table in the future state.
The existing database should be pressure-tested to identify duplicate or overlapping functionality.
Before creating new infrastructure or backend functionality, establish a solid business/technical reason that the capability must remain separate.
Consolidation may involve trade-offs, so those trade-offs should be explicitly documented.
Avoid creating multiple backend implementations of the same enterprise capability.
D. Shared Enterprise Platform

The desired Oil Console architecture should provide reusable shared capabilities for future applications.

Potential shared capabilities include:

Files/documents
Comments
Notifications
Work items / To-Dos
Approvals
Entity relationships
Entity-level permissions
Agent runs

The preferred principle is:

Duplicate UI where necessary, but avoid duplicating backend functionality and data models.

E. Universal Entity Graph

The future architecture should explore a universal entity graph as a common foundation.

The graph could connect entities such as:

Vendors
Carriers
Goals
Knowledge Base articles
Contracts
Spend
Other future first-class entities

A shared/polymorphic relationship model should allow the platform to determine how entities are related without creating separate relationship implementations for every application.

F. Entity-Level Authorization

The proposed authorization model should support permissions on a specific first-class entity record, rather than only application-wide roles.

Conceptually:

User/Team → Entity Contributor → Entity Record → Role → Permissions

Potential roles include:

Viewer
Editor
Publisher

The model should support both:

Individual users
Teams/groups

This allows, for example, an entire EXL team to receive viewer/editor access to specific records while team membership automatically determines inherited access.

The authorization hierarchy and permission model need to be explored in greater detail beyond the current basic access/PDPP implementation.

G. Unified Work / Notification Experience

The long-term goal is for users to have one central location for work requiring attention, rather than checking multiple applications.

Examples include:

TOAP item movements
Knowledge Base article approvals
Carrier creation
Agent/HITL decisions
Future application workflows

These should ultimately surface through a common work/task experience and be paired with appropriate notifications.

The NerveHub concept was referenced as an example of a centralized location for work and process tracking.

H. Centralized Agent Runs

Agent executions should potentially use a shared Agent Run model.

An Agent Run could capture:

Unique run ID
Agent
Session
User
Status
Related entity/entities
Execution information

The relationship between agent runs and entities should support the same shared/polymorphic architecture principles.

I. Salesforce Future State

The team should consider the possibility that Salesforce functionality could eventually be replaced by another application within the Oil Console/monorepo.

Therefore, the future architecture should avoid creating unnecessary dependencies that assume Salesforce will always remain part of the platform.

J. Migration Scope
The current implementation remains narrowly scoped to the original applications, TOAP, and Knowledge Base migration.
Development of POCs, agents, vendor applications, carrier applications, etc. should continue.
The future-state architecture should guide those efforts but should not unnecessarily expand the immediate migration scope.
The first two migrated applications should act as the reference implementation / guiding light for future applications.
